# .gitattributes — line-ending + byte-integrity policy for the ffiec repo.
#
# Two hazard classes are guarded here. Both are the same class of bug
# Heather guarded in ffiec-public (commit 18e7c32, `spec/test-vectors/**
# -text`): a fresh clone on a machine with core.autocrlf=true rewrites LF
# to CRLF on checkout and silently changes the bytes a tool reads.
#
# 1. gofmt-friendliness. Go source MUST stay LF. gofmt emits LF, `go vet`
#    and the toolchain assume LF, and a CRLF-rewritten .go file shows up
#    as a whole-file diff against a Unix contributor. `text eol=lf` pins
#    the working-tree bytes to LF regardless of the cloner's autocrlf.
#
# 2. byte-pinned crypto material. The conformance corpus lives in
#    ffiec-public, but any signing-key material, hex digests, signatures,
#    or golden fixtures vendored INTO this repo must be byte-for-byte
#    identical on every clone. The verifier consumes ONLY public-key
#    material (never the private seed); the test keypair lives outside the
#    repo under TESSERASEAL_TEST_KEY_DIR. Should a .hex/.pem/.sig/.bin or
#    a *.golden land here in future, `-text` makes Git treat it as binary
#    so no EOL normalization runs on it — a CRLF-mangled fingerprint or
#    signature would fail verification loudly, but the better outcome is
#    that the mangling never happens. This is the every-bug-is-a-class
#    guard: pin the class now, before the first such file is committed.

# Default: let Git auto-detect text vs binary and normalize text to LF in
# the repo. Explicit rules below override this for the load-bearing cases.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Class 1: Go toolchain source (gofmt requires LF) ---
*.go        text eol=lf
go.mod      text eol=lf
go.sum      text eol=lf
go.work     text eol=lf

# Repo prose + config: LF for cross-platform diff stability.
*.md        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.ndjson    text eol=lf

# --- Class 2: byte-pinned crypto material + golden fixtures ---
# Treated as binary (-text): no EOL normalization, ever. A CRLF rewrite
# of a key fingerprint, a signature, or a golden compare would corrupt
# the verify. None of these are tracked today; the guard pins the class.
*.hex       -text
*.pem       -text
*.sig       -text
*.bin       -text
*.golden    -text
